{
  "rationale": "Evidence: iteration 2 (score 442.8) is still the best design. Its oval finishes 15/20 and its s-bend 3/20, but the slalom never finishes (progress 0.33, max_error about 0.063 m, just over the fixed 0.06 m off-track limit). Iteration 3 scored lower (418.4): base_speed 1.0, a wider span (+/-0.06 m), and kp 2.6 cut slalom progress to 0.16 and s-bend finishes to 2/20. So the wider span with lower per-metre gain and the lower speed did not help. The off-track limit is max(0.06, 0.0125 + body_width/4), which is 0.06 m for every allowed body width, so tracking error has to fall. Iteration 3 also showed the robot is limited by steering response on tight alternating bends, not by speed.\n\nChanges, all relative to iteration 2: (1) wheel_track 0.12 to 0.10 m. Turn rate is the wheel speed difference divided by track, so the same turn command gives about 20 percent more yaw rate. This addresses the failure to follow tight alternating bends. (2) mass 0.6 to 0.5 kg, which lowers yaw inertia and speeds up correction. (3) kp 2.0 to 1.8 to offset the higher effective steering gain from the narrower track and avoid oscillation. (4) Sensors, weights, kd 0.6, ki 0.05, base_speed 1.2 and line_loss last_direction are kept from iteration 2, so the effect of the geometry change is isolated.\n\nExpected result: slalom progress well above 0.33 and max_error nearer 0.05 m, with the oval and s-bend completion rate at least held near iteration 2 levels. Hypotheses: H5, steering authority (turn rate per command) is the limit on the slalom, so a narrower track should raise slalom progress. H6, lower mass and yaw inertia reduce the lag that causes line loss on alternating bends. Risks: a narrower track with the same kp may increase oscillation on the oval (steering_oscillation was 0.38 in iteration 2). Measurements wanted next: progress and completion_time per track to check whether oval finish time holds, max_error and line_loss_events on the slalom to test H5, and steering_oscillation to check for jitter from the higher turn gain.",
